Scaler FDE curriculum gaps

Comparison with employer requirements

Prepared for Praneeth Karnatakam | 17 November 2026

Recommendation

Prioritise reusable customer solutions, product feedback, practical model adaptation and delivery across unfamiliar platforms. Strengthen assessment of integration, debugging, AI quality and handover. Scaler already covers the principal RAG, agent, software and cloud concepts; the advanced track should make the expected depth and passing evidence explicit.

How to interpret the evidence

The research contains 104 selected job descriptions from 104 employers, including 16 India-listed roles. Technology and capability totals were reconciled against the workbook skill tags. It is a purposive global sample with a large US share, not a market census or an India-only demand estimate.

Role-linked counts include core qualifications, preferences and delivery responsibilities, and exclude context-only mentions. Qualification lists may contain examples or alternatives. Categories overlap, so counts must not be added as unique employers. Capability groupings are analyst interpretations of employer wording.

A gap means a topic or required demonstration is not explicit in the published Scaler outline. It does not establish that instructors never teach it. The assessments below are recommendations derived from the comparison, not verbatim employer requirements.

Highest priority additions

Reusable customer solutions

65/104 descriptions mention reusable tooling or patterns. Reusable Python components are present, but reuse across customer engagements deserves explicit treatment.

Required demonstration Convert a Meridian-specific connector into a configurable package. Deploy it for a second customer without copying the codebase; document configuration, versioning and tests.

Product feedback from deployments

39/104 mention product feedback. A structured customer-to-product feedback loop is not explicit.

Required demonstration Produce a reproducible customer issue, identify whether it is general or customer-specific, and propose a product change with acceptance criteria.

Practical model adaptation

Fine-tuning/post-training: 16 mentions, 14 role-linked. Scaler discusses when to fine-tune; a complete assessed training-and-comparison exercise is not explicit.

Required demonstration Prepare data, run one bounded adaptation experiment, compare against prompt/RAG baselines and defend adopting or rejecting the result.

Further additions and stronger assessments

Delivery beyond the primary cloud

GCP: 22 mentions, 17 role-linked. Azure: 15 mentions, 14 role-linked. Scaler includes cross-cloud mapping; practical migration depth is unclear.

Required demonstration Deploy a small service on one alternate cloud and explain identity, networking, model-service and operational differences.

Agent context and memory

4 mentions, all classified as core qualifications; some list examples or alternatives. Persistent memory lifecycle and context management are not explicitly named.

Required demonstration Implement task state, selective context retrieval, tenant-separated memory, expiry and deletion.

Unfamiliar customer technology stacks

Go: 16 mentions, 15 role-linked. Java: 13 role-linked mentions. The main teaching stack does not establish experience changing an unfamiliar customer service.

Required demonstration Diagnose and patch a supplied Java or Go service, then integrate it with the Python solution. Assess reading and adaptation rather than mastery of every language.

Existing coverage requiring stronger evidence

These are assessment improvements, not absent subject areas. The workbook supports the broad capabilities; the specific failure exercises are curriculum recommendations.

